module lib/gpt/gpt  documents every variable
  A GPT is a model that reads a sequence of tokens and, at every position,
  gives each token of its vocabulary a probability of coming next. Here a
  token is one character and the vocabulary is the set of characters in the
  training text. The model is the one nanoGPT and the modded-nanogpt speedrun
  train, cut down to a size the interpreter trains in seconds.

  Each token becomes a row of `width` numbers: its row of the token embedding
  plus its position's row of the position embedding. The rows of a sequence
  form the residual stream, a matrix with one row per position. Each block
  reads the stream and adds two things to it. Self-attention lets each
  position take a weighted average of information from itself and the
  positions before it, never the ones after. The MLP (multilayer perceptron)
  works on each position alone, through a wider layer and back. After the
  last block, a normalization and one matrix turn each row into a logit per
  vocabulary entry, and a softmax of the logits is the prediction.

  The model is a record of its matrices, laid out as nanoGPT's modules are:
  a `Gpt` holds the embeddings, its `Block`s and the output map; a block
  holds its attention (a `Head` of query, key and value matrices per head,
  and the output projection) and its MLP. Training lowers the cross-entropy
  between the predictions and the characters that come next. A training step
  runs the model on a whole batch of sequences at once: the stream is then a
  stack of matrices, one per sequence, and `@` and the reductions work on
  each matrix of the stack. The gradient is `value_and_grad` of the batch's
  loss with respect to the model: a `Gpt` of the same shape, filled by the
  backward pass the compiler writes from the forward pass; the explorer shows the forward pass inside it as the
  defs here. The update is the speedrun's split: Muon for
  the matrices inside the blocks, Adam for the embeddings and the output map,
  each matrix updated in place.

init_matrix  @b13b4343
  what  A matrix of normal random numbers scaled by $1/\sqrt{\text{rows}}$.
  why   The scale keeps a matrix product's entries about as large as its
        input's, whatever the width.
  rows  its rows: the width of what it multiplies
  cols  its columns

Head  @c61322ca
  what  One head's query, key and value matrices, drawn in that order, each
        width by width / heads.
  c     the model's sizes
  hd    the width of one head

Block  @0226da76
  what   One block's starting matrices, drawn in the order the speedrun's
         layout lists them: each head's query, key and value (the heads one
         after another), the output projection, the MLP's two matrices.
  c      the model's sizes
  attn   the block's attention
  mlp    the block's MLP

Gpt  @f4a74658
  what    The starting model: the embeddings normal random numbers, unscaled,
          every block's matrices from `Block`, and the output map zero.
  why     A zero output map makes every logit zero, so the first prediction
          is uniform over the vocabulary and the first loss is $\log V$
          exactly: the loss of a guess.
  c       the model's sizes
  tok     the token embedding, vocab by width: one row per token
  pos     the position embedding, context by width: one row per position
  blocks  the blocks, in the order the stream passes through them

count_into  @55c63807
  what  Add a matrix's number of entries to `n[0]`: what `gpt_size` calls on
        each matrix of the model.
  w     a matrix of the model
  n     the count so far, in its one entry

gpt_size  @94d90dce
  what  The number of the model's parameters: the entries of all its matrices.
  m     the model
  n     the count, in its one entry

self_attention  @2d2dd5ee
  what    Causal self-attention with several heads. Each head attends on its own,
          with its own query, key and value matrices of width D / heads; their
          outputs sit side by side in Z, and the output projection mixes them
          back into width D.
  how     lib/nn's attention computes each head for every sequence of the
          batch at once, and its output goes into its own columns of Z.
  why     Several narrow heads can each look at a different position for a
          different reason, one at the character before and one further back,
          for the arithmetic of one wide head.
  a       the block's attention: its heads and its output projection
  N       the normalized stream, B by T by D: what attention reads
  hd      the width of one head
  Z       the heads' outputs side by side, B by T by D, before the output projection
  i       the head's place among the heads, which picks its columns of Z
  h       its query, key and value matrices, each D by hd
  Y       attention's output, B by T by D, which the block adds to the stream

  for each i
    head     run one head and put its output in its own columns of Z
  project  mix the heads' outputs with the output projection

block  @0fbc10e7
  what    One transformer block: normalize the stream and attend, add the result
          to the stream; normalize again and run the MLP, add that too.
  how     Pre-norm: each branch reads a normalized copy of the stream, and its
          output is added to the stream as it is. Each step is one line of the
          formula below, and `X` holds $x$, then $x_1$, then $x_2$.
  why     Adding to the stream instead of replacing it (the residual) lets each
          block make a small change, and on the way back it gives the gradient a
          path around every branch. Normalizing a branch's input fixes its scale
          however large the stream grows.
  b       the block's matrices
  X       $x$  the stream, B by T by D: as it enters the block, after
          attention's output is added, and after the MLP's

  $$ x_1 = x + \mathrm{Attn}(\mathrm{norm}(x)), \qquad x_2 = x_1 + \mathrm{MLP}(\mathrm{norm}(x_1)) $$

  attention  normalize the stream, attend over its rows, and add the result to the stream
  mlp        normalize the new stream, run the MLP (widen to hidden, apply ReLU squared, come back to width), and add its output
    $$ \mathrm{MLP}(n) = \max(n W_{\mathrm{up}}, 0)^2 \, W_{\mathrm{down}} $$

gpt_forward  @8db5167e
  what    The logits for every position of a batch of token sequences: embed,
          run the blocks in order, normalize, map each row to one logit per
          vocabulary entry.
  m       the model
  tokens  the sequences, batch by positions: one token index per position
  X       the residual stream, B by T by D
  b       the block being run
  logits  B by T by vocab: row t of a sequence scores every token as the one
          after position t

  embed       each token's row of the token embedding plus its position's row of the position embedding
    why         Attention and the MLP treat every position alike. The position embedding is the only place the model can learn where a token sits, and without it "lo" and "ol" would look the same to attention.
  for each b
    block       run the stream through the block
  logits      normalize the last stream and map each row to one logit per vocabulary entry

sequence_loss  @e4a4b838
  what    The mean cross-entropy of a batch of sequences against their
          targets, over every position of every sequence: the function
          training differentiates.
  how     `train` calls `value_and_grad(sequence_loss, model, ...)`, which runs
          this forward pass, keeps what the backward pass needs, and runs the
          backward pass the compiler wrote for it: from the loss to the logits,
          back through the output map, the final normalization and the blocks,
          last block first, to the embeddings, where each position's gradient
          goes to the one row of each table that position looked up. Each
          matrix's gradient lands at its own place in a `Gpt` of the model's
          shape.
  m       the model
  x       the input tokens, batch by positions
  y       the targets: at each position, the token that comes next in the text
  logits  B by T by V
  loss    the mean over positions and sequences of $-\log p$, with p the
          probability the model gave the right next token

  forward  run the model
  loss     score the logits against the next tokens

optimizer_step  @a4d42823
  what      One update of every matrix of the model from the gradient: Muon for
            each matrix inside the blocks, Adam for the embeddings and the output
            map.
  how       `for_leaves` calls `muon_update` (lib/optim) on each matrix of the
            blocks with the same matrix of the gradient and of the momentum;
            `adam_update` (lib/optim) takes the other three. `GPT_MUON` and
            `GPT_ADAM` hold the step sizes and decay rates.
  model     the model, its matrices changed in place
  g         $G$  the gradient, a `Gpt` of the model's shape, averaged over the batch
  m         the optimizer's first memory, of the model's shape: Muon's momentum
            for the blocks, Adam's average gradient for the rest
  v         the second: Adam's average squared gradient
  taken     how many updates have been made, this one included
  lr_scale  the fraction of the starting step sizes the schedule allows now

  muon  momentum, orthogonalize it, step along it, for every matrix of the blocks
  adam  running averages, bias corrected, step by their ratio, for the rest

batch_windows  @96c8a87a
  what     The sequences of training step t: `batch` windows of `context` tokens
           cut from the text, each step's windows starting where the last
           step's left off. With a shift of one token, the same windows moved
           one token on: the targets.
  tokens   the whole text as tokens
  c        the model's sizes
  t        the training step
  shift    `0` for the inputs, `1` for the targets
  starts   where each window starts in the text, one per row of the batch;
           window b of step t starts at t * batch + b, wrapped to the text

train  @9ee69166
  what      Train the model in place for `steps` steps, and return the mean loss
            of each step's batch, measured before the step's update.
  how       Each step cuts a batch of windows, takes the batch's loss and
            gradient from one `value_and_grad` over the whole batch, and
            updates the model. The step sizes fall in a straight line from
            their starting values toward `LR_FLOOR` of them.
  why       The loss is measured on the batch the step trains on, so it is
            noisy: windows that start at different characters are not equally
            easy. A falling step size lets the last steps settle instead of
            stepping over the minimum.
  model     the model, changed in place
  c         its sizes; the batch size is read here
  tokens    the training text as tokens
  steps     how many steps to take
  losses    one entry per step
  m         the optimizer's first memory, of the model's shape
  v         its second memory
  t         the step
  xs        the batch's inputs, batch by context
  ys        the batch's targets: the same windows one token on
  loss      the batch's mean loss
  grad      the gradient of the batch's mean loss, a `Gpt` of the model's shape
  gnorm     the length of the gradient over all the model's matrices
  lr_scale  the schedule's fraction of the starting step sizes

  for each t
    batch             cut this step's windows, and the targets one token on
    forward_backward  the batch's loss and its gradient
    measure           the length of the gradient
    update            one optimizer step at the scheduled step size

sample  @f720937f
  what    Continue the prompt by count tokens, each drawn from the model's
          probabilities given the last context tokens written so far.
  why     Drawing from the probabilities, instead of always taking the most likely
          token, is how a GPT is sampled; a model that has learned the text puts
          nearly all the probability on the right character, and the draws
          follow it.
  m       the model
  prompt  the tokens to continue
  count   how many tokens to add
  seed    the random generator's seed for the draws
  n       the context length: the position embedding's rows
  out     the prompt followed by the tokens drawn so far
  k       the token being drawn
  ctx     the last n tokens of out, or all of them while there are fewer
  p       the probability of each token coming next: the softmax of the
          logits at the last position, from the model run on a batch of one
          sequence

  for each k
    context  take the last context tokens written so far
    probs    the model's probabilities for the next token
    draw     draw a token from them and append it

encode  @313025f1
  what    A text as tokens: each character replaced by its place in the
